 "note": "ARRIVAL IS \"THE NEXT OCCUPANT\" -- the reading, and the two readings rejected.**\nBoth obvious anchors are VACUOUS, and this was measured, not argued\n(`analysis/stand-clip-vacuous.json`, `logs/standclip-v1.log`):\n\n  * the base head's own `stand_next_arr_inblock_dt` (`exp-018-fresh/code/features.py:264`)\n    is the first same-stand arrival at or AFTER the take-off, so a violation would need an\n    off-block after an instant that is itself at or after the take-off -- a negative taxi.\n    Measured on the three folds: 79,804 / 49,333 / 63,222 no-clock rows in scope and\n    **0 label violations** in all three;\n  * \"the first arrival strictly after the last one at or before take-off\" is vacuous for\n    the same reason -- the last arrival at or before take-off absorbs any intruder, so the\n    next one is always after the take-off.  Measured: 79,534 / 49,138 / 62,874 rows in\n    scope, **0** violations, and **0** rows where that arrival even falls before take-off.\n\nThe constraint has content in exactly one configuration: an arrival takes the stand\nBEFORE our take-off, which it can only do after we push back.  Call `Q` the last\nsame-stand arrival in-block at or before the take-off and `dt = take-off - Q`.  If `Q`\nwere the row's OWN inbound, `dt` would be the whole ground time, so a SMALL `dt` says `Q`\ncannot be the row's own inbound -- no aircraft arrives, turns round, pushes back and taxis\nout inside `TURN_MIN` -- and `Q` is therefore the next occupant.  Then the stand ordering\nsays the off-block is before `Q`, that is **taxi >= dt**: a label-free LOWER bound, and\nthe mirror of the base head's `stand_prev_arr_inblock_dt`, which the same file calls a\n\"hard-ish upper bound on the taxi time\" and reads as a FEATURE, never as a constraint.\n`TURN_MIN` is swept (10 / 15 / 20 / 30 min) instead of chosen, because the whole reading\nrests on it.",
